GUIDES = [
dict(slug="small-living-room-layout", title="How to Lay Out a Small Living Room: Measurements That Actually Work",
desc="Exact clearances, furniture sizes and layout patterns that make a small living room feel larger and work harder.",
cat="Layout", read=8, project="living", body="""
<p>Most small living rooms don't fail because they are small — they fail because the furniture is the wrong size for the circulation the room needs. Before you buy anything, measure, sketch and test. This guide gives you the numbers designers use.</p>
<div class="toc"><strong>In this guide</strong><ol><li><a href="#measure">Measure the room properly</a></li><li><a href="#clearances">The clearances that matter</a></li><li><a href="#layouts">Four layouts for small rooms</a></li><li><a href="#scale">Choosing furniture at the right scale</a></li><li><a href="#tricks">Visual tricks that add space</a></li></ol></div>
<h2 id="measure">1. Measure the room properly</h2>
<p>Record the length and width of every wall, the position and swing of each door, window sill heights, radiators, outlets and any TV or cable points. Mark the main paths people walk — from the entry to the seating, to the balcony, to the kitchen. Those paths are fixed; furniture has to work around them.</p>
<p>Drop the dimensions into our <a href="../planner.html">free room planner</a> and add furniture at real size before you shop. Ten minutes on a plan saves a costly return.</p>
<h2 id="clearances">2. The clearances that matter</h2>
<table><tr><th>Clearance</th><th>Comfortable</th><th>Minimum</th></tr>
<tr><td>Main walkway</td><td>36&quot; (91 cm)</td><td>30&quot; (76 cm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sofa to coffee table</td><td>16–18&quot; (40–46 cm)</td><td>14&quot; (36 cm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seat to seat for conversation</td><td>4–8 ft (1.2–2.4 m)</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>TV viewing distance (4K)</td><td>1–1.5 × screen diagonal</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Behind a chair that's used</td><td>24–30&quot; (61–76 cm)</td><td>22&quot;</td></tr></table>
<p>Our <a href="../tools.html#clearance">furniture fit checker</a> calculates what's left once a piece is in place.</p>
<h2 id="layouts">3. Four layouts for small rooms</h2>
<h3>The floating L</h3><p>A compact sectional pulled slightly off the wall with a console behind it. It defines a seating zone, leaves a walkway behind, and works especially well in open-plan apartments.</p>
<h3>Facing pair</h3><p>Two loveseats or a sofa and two chairs facing each other across a coffee table. It's the best conversation layout and keeps sightlines low, but needs a room at least 11 ft (3.4 m) wide.</p>
<h3>Wall-hugger with swivel chairs</h3><p>Sofa against the longest wall, two swivel chairs opposite. Swivels let you face the TV or the conversation, and their compact footprint keeps the walkway open.</p>
<h3>Daybed + chairs</h3><p>For very narrow rooms, a backless daybed can be approached from either side and doubles as a guest bed.</p>
<h2 id="scale">4. Choosing furniture at the right scale</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Sofa depth:</strong> 32–36&quot; instead of the 40&quot;+ deep \"cloud\" styles.</li><li><strong>Arms:</strong> track or slim arms can save 8–12&quot; of width versus rolled arms.</li><li><strong>Legs:</strong> raised furniture shows more floor, which reads as more space.</li><li><strong>Coffee table:</strong> about two-thirds the length of the sofa; round or oval tables ease tight walkways.</li><li><strong>Rug:</strong> large enough that the front legs of all seating sit on it. Undersized rugs make rooms look smaller. Use the <a href="../tools.html#rug">rug size calculator</a>.</li></ul>
<h2 id="tricks">5. Visual tricks that add space</h2>
<ul><li>Hang curtains high and wide — 4–6&quot; above the frame and 8–10&quot; beyond each side.</li><li>Keep walls, curtains and larger upholstery in related tones so edges blur.</li><li>Use a large mirror opposite or beside a window to bounce daylight.</li><li>Choose glass, acrylic or open-frame side tables.</li><li>Light the corners: dark corners shrink a room.</li></ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Keep total furniture footprint at or below about 50–60% of the floor area. The planner shows this percentage live.</div>
<p>Still stuck? <a href="../get-quote.html?project=living">Get matched with a designer</a> who can produce a scaled layout for your exact room.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="kitchen-remodel-cost", title="Kitchen Remodel Cost Guide: What You'll Really Spend and Where",
desc="A practical breakdown of kitchen renovation budgets — cost tiers, where the money goes, resale value and ways to save.",
cat="Budget", read=10, project="kitchen", body="""
<p>The kitchen is the most expensive room per square foot to renovate, and the easiest to overspend on. Use this guide to set a realistic budget before you talk to contractors.</p>
<div class="callout">Every figure here is a broad planning range in USD. Labour rates, codes and material prices vary widely by region — always get at least three written quotes. Try our <a href="../tools.html#budget">renovation budget estimator</a> for a room-specific range.</div>
<h2>Three budget tiers</h2>
<table><tr><th>Scope</th><th>What it usually includes</th><th>Typical range</th></tr>
<tr><td>Refresh</td><td>Paint, cabinet refacing or painting, new hardware, lighting, backsplash, one or two appliances</td><td>$10,000–$30,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mid-range remodel</td><td>New semi-custom cabinets, quartz or solid-surface counters, mid-range appliances, new flooring, same layout</td><td>$35,000–$80,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Major / upscale</td><td>Layout changes, moving plumbing and gas, custom cabinetry, premium appliances, structural work</td><td>$90,000+</td></tr></table>
<h2>Where the money goes</h2>
<p>A common rule of thumb for a full remodel:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Cabinets & hardware:</strong> ~30%</li><li><strong>Labour & installation:</strong> ~20%</li><li><strong>Appliances & ventilation:</strong> ~15%</li><li><strong>Countertops:</strong> ~10%</li><li><strong>Flooring, lighting, plumbing fixtures, backsplash:</strong> ~15%</li><li><strong>Design, permits, contingency:</strong> ~10%</li></ul>
<h2>The decisions that move the number most</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Keeping the layout.</strong> Moving a sink, range or gas line triggers plumbing, electrical and often permit and inspection costs. Keeping the footprint is the single biggest saver.</li><li><strong>Cabinet grade.</strong> Stock, semi-custom and custom cabinetry can differ by a factor of three or more for the same run.</li><li><strong>Counter material.</strong> Laminate, quartz, granite, natural marble and sintered stone span a wide price band per square foot.</li><li><strong>Appliance package.</strong> Pro-style ranges and panel-ready refrigerators can cost more than an entire refresh.</li></ol>
<h2>Resale value</h2>
<p>Industry cost-versus-value studies have consistently found that modest, mid-range kitchen updates recover a much larger share of their cost at resale than upscale major remodels. If you plan to sell within a few years, spend on cabinets fronts, lighting, counters and appliances buyers see, not hidden luxury.</p>
<h2>Ten ways to save without looking cheap</h2>
<ul><li>Refinish or reface sound cabinet boxes instead of replacing them.</li><li>Mix open shelving with fewer upper cabinets.</li><li>Use a premium material only on the island, a cheaper one on the perimeter.</li><li>Buy floor-model or previous-year appliances.</li><li>Choose standard cabinet sizes.</li><li>Do demolition and painting yourself if you're able.</li><li>Order everything before demolition to avoid idle-trade days.</li><li>Use large-format tile — fewer grout lines, faster install.</li><li>Upgrade lighting: under-cabinet LEDs change a kitchen dramatically for little cost.</li><li>Keep a 10–20% contingency so surprises don't force downgrades later.</li></ul>
<h2>Timeline</h2><p>Plan 2–4 weeks for design and ordering, 4–12 weeks of construction for a mid-range remodel, and longer for custom cabinets or permits.</p>
<p>Ready to price it properly? <a href="../get-quote.html?project=kitchen">Get free, no-obligation quotes from kitchen pros</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="hire-interior-designer", title="How to Hire an Interior Designer: Fees, Questions and Red Flags",
desc="Understand designer fee structures, what to ask before signing, and how to tell a great fit from an expensive mistake.",
cat="Hiring", read=9, project="full-home", body="""
<p>A good designer saves money by preventing mistakes, unlocking trade pricing and managing trades. A poor fit costs time and money. Here's how to choose well.</p>
<h2>Designer, decorator, or e-designer?</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Interior designer:</strong> plans space, finishes, lighting and often coordinates construction drawings. In some regions the title is regulated and requires certification.</li><li><strong>Decorator:</strong> focuses on furnishings, colour, textiles and styling — no structural work.</li><li><strong>E-designer / online design service:</strong> remote concept boards, floor plans and shopping lists, usually flat-fee per room. Great value for a single room or tight budgets.</li></ul>
<h2>How designers charge</h2>
<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>How it works</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hourly</td><td>Commonly $100–$300+/hour depending on market and experience</td><td>Consultations, small projects</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flat fee per room</td><td>Fixed price per room or package; online services often a few hundred dollars per room</td><td>Clear, defined scope</td></tr>
<tr><td>Percentage of project</td><td>Often 10–30% of total project cost</td><td>Full renovations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost-plus</td><td>Designer buys at trade price and adds a markup</td><td>Furnishing-heavy projects</td></tr></table>
<p>Ask exactly what's included: number of concepts, revisions, site visits, drawings, procurement and installation day.</p>
<h2>Twelve questions to ask before signing</h2>
<ol><li>Can I see completed projects similar to mine in size and budget?</li><li>Who will I work with day-to-day?</li><li>How do you charge, and what triggers extra fees?</li><li>Do you pass trade discounts on to clients?</li><li>How many revision rounds are included?</li><li>Will you produce scaled floor plans and elevations?</li><li>Do you manage contractors, or recommend them?</li><li>What's a realistic timeline from concept to install?</li><li>How do you handle back-orders and damaged deliveries?</li><li>Are you insured and, where relevant, licensed?</li><li>Can I speak with two recent clients?</li><li>What happens if we part ways mid-project?</li></ol>
<h2>Red flags</h2><ul><li>No written contract or a vague scope.</li><li>Pressure to pay large deposits on furniture before plans are approved.</li><li>Portfolio that's all renderings with no completed work.</li><li>Unwillingness to discuss budget openly.</li></ul>
<h2>Preparing for your first meeting</h2>
<p>Bring room measurements (or a <a href="../planner.html">plan from our planner</a>), photos, a budget range, a list of what stays, and 10–20 images you love. Our <a href="../quiz.html">style quiz</a> gives you a vocabulary for your taste in two minutes.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Skip the search:</strong> <a href="../get-quote.html">tell us about your space</a> and we'll connect you with designers who match your style, budget and location — free and with no obligation.</div>
"""),

dict(slug="home-office-design", title="Home Office Design: Ergonomics, Light and Layout for Real Productivity",
desc="Design a home office that protects your back, eyes and focus — desk heights, monitor placement, lighting, acoustics and storage.",
cat="Work", read=8, project="office", body="""
<p>A productive home office is mostly about three things: posture, light and fewer distractions. Style comes after.</p>
<h2>Ergonomics by the numbers</h2>
<table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Guideline</th></tr>
<tr><td>Desk height (seated)</td><td>Elbows at about 90°, usually 28–30&quot; (71–76 cm) for average adults; adjustable is best</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monitor</td><td>About an arm's length away, top line at or slightly below eye level</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chair</td><td>Feet flat, thighs roughly parallel to the floor, lumbar support</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desk depth</td><td>At least 24&quot; (61 cm); 30&quot; for large monitors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chair clearance behind desk</td><td>36&quot; (91 cm) to push back and stand comfortably</td></tr></table>
<h2>Where to put the desk</h2>
<p>Place the desk perpendicular to a window so daylight comes from the side — facing the window causes glare and squinting, and putting your back to it puts glare on the screen. If you take video calls, face the light source and keep a tidy, simple background behind you.</p>
<h2>Light it in layers</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Ambient:</strong> overhead or indirect light so the room isn't a cave.</li><li><strong>Task:</strong> an adjustable desk lamp placed opposite your writing hand.</li><li><strong>Colour temperature:</strong> 3500–4000K keeps you alert; switch to warmer light in the evening.</li></ul>
<p>Use our <a href="../tools.html#lighting">lumens calculator</a> — offices typically need 30–50 foot-candles of light.</p>
<h2>Sound and focus</h2><ul><li>Soft surfaces — rugs, curtains, upholstered chairs, bookcases — absorb echo.</li><li>A door sweep and a solid-core door help more than any gadget.</li><li>Keep only today's work on the desk; everything else gets a closed home.</li></ul>
<h2>Small-space solutions</h2><ul><li>Wall-mounted fold-down desks for hallways and bedrooms.</li><li>A closet conversion (\"cloffice\") with shelves above and a 24&quot;-deep worktop.</li><li>A console desk behind a sofa doubles as a room divider.</li></ul>
<p>Want it done for you? <a href="../get-quote.html?project=office">Get matched with a workspace designer</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="bathroom-renovation-planning", title="Bathroom Renovation Planning: Layout, Clearances and Budget",
desc="Plan a bathroom remodel the right way — fixture clearances, ventilation, waterproofing, materials and realistic costs.",
cat="Renovation", read=9, project="bathroom", body="""
<p>Bathrooms pack plumbing, electrical, ventilation and waterproofing into a tiny footprint. Get the plan right and everything else is easier.</p>
<h2>Fixture clearances</h2>
<table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Recommended</th><th>Common code minimum*</th></tr>
<tr><td>Toilet centreline to side wall/fixture</td><td>18&quot; (46 cm)</td><td>15&quot; (38 cm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clear space in front of toilet</td><td>30&quot; (76 cm)</td><td>21&quot; (53 cm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>In front of vanity</td><td>30&quot;+</td><td>21&quot;</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shower interior</td><td>36 × 36&quot; (91 cm)</td><td>30 × 30&quot;</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vanity height</td><td>32–36&quot; (81–91 cm)</td><td>—</td></tr></table>
<p class="muted">*Codes vary by country, state and municipality. Confirm local requirements before building.</p>
<h2>Keep the wet wall</h2><p>Moving a toilet drain is one of the most expensive bathroom changes. Arranging fixtures along the existing plumbing wall keeps cost and risk down.</p>
<h2>Waterproofing and ventilation are non-negotiable</h2><ul><li>Use a proper waterproof membrane behind tile in showers — tile and grout alone are not waterproof.</li><li>Size the exhaust fan to the room and duct it outside, never into the attic.</li><li>Slope shower floors toward the drain; consider linear drains for curbless showers.</li></ul>
<h2>Materials that last</h2><ul><li>Porcelain tile: dense, low-absorption, great for floors and showers.</li><li>Quartz vanity tops: low-maintenance, stain-resistant.</li><li>Solid-brass valves and cartridges: worth the premium inside walls.</li><li>Large-format tiles: fewer grout lines to clean.</li></ul>
<h2>Budget ranges</h2><p>A cosmetic refresh (vanity, toilet, lighting, paint, mirror) is often in the low five figures or less; a full gut with new tile, glass and fixtures typically costs considerably more, especially in high-cost cities. Run your numbers through our <a href="../tools.html#budget">budget estimator</a>, then <a href="../get-quote.html?project=bathroom">collect three quotes</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Accessibility tip:</strong> Install blocking in the walls for future grab bars while they're open — it costs little now and a lot later.</div>
"""),

dict(slug="lighting-design-basics", title="Lighting Design Basics: Layers, Lumens and Colour Temperature",
desc="Learn ambient, task and accent lighting, how many lumens each room needs and how to choose bulbs that flatter your space.",
cat="Lighting", read=7, project="lighting", body="""
<p>Lighting changes how big, warm and expensive a room feels more than almost any other upgrade — and it's one of the cheapest to get right.</p>
<h2>The three layers</h2><ul><li><strong>Ambient</strong> — the general fill: ceiling fixtures, recessed lights, cove lighting.</li><li><strong>Task</strong> — focused light for reading, cooking and grooming: desk lamps, under-cabinet strips, vanity sconces.</li><li><strong>Accent</strong> — highlights art, shelves and texture: picture lights, uplights, LED tape.</li></ul><p>A well-lit room uses all three and puts them on separate switches or dimmers.</p>
<h2>How many lumens?</h2>
<table><tr><th>Room</th><th>Foot-candles</th><th>Example: 150 sq ft room</th></tr>
<tr><td>Living / bedroom</td><td>10–20</td><td>1,500–3,000 lm</td></tr><tr><td>Dining</td><td>30–40</td><td>4,500–6,000 lm</td></tr><tr><td>Kitchen</td><td>30–40</td><td>4,500–6,000 lm</td></tr><tr><td>Home office</td><td>30–50</td><td>4,500–7,500 lm</td></tr><tr><td>Bathroom</td><td>70–80</td><td>10,500–12,000 lm</td></tr></table>
<p>Multiply room square footage by foot-candles — or let our <a href="../tools.html#lighting">lumens calculator</a> do it.</p>
<h2>Colour temperature (Kelvin)</h2><ul><li><strong>2700K:</strong> warm, cosy — living rooms and bedrooms.</li><li><strong>3000K:</strong> warm-neutral — kitchens and bathrooms that still feel inviting.</li><li><strong>3500–4000K:</strong> crisp — offices, garages, laundry.</li></ul><p>Keep one colour temperature per room so surfaces don't look patchy.</p>
<h2>CRI matters</h2><p>Colour Rendering Index measures how accurately a light shows colours. Choose CRI 90+ in kitchens, bathrooms and anywhere you choose paint or clothes.</p>
<h2>Placement rules of thumb</h2><ul><li>Pendants over an island: 30–36&quot; above the counter.</li><li>Chandelier over a dining table: about 30–34&quot; above the tabletop, roughly half to two-thirds the table width.</li><li>Vanity lights: at face height on both sides of the mirror beats a single bar overhead.</li><li>Recessed lights: start about 2–3 ft from walls to avoid harsh scalloping.</li></ul>
<p><a href="../get-quote.html?project=lighting">Talk to a lighting designer</a> for a full plan.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="color-scheme-guide", title="How to Choose a Colour Scheme for Your Home (Without Regret)",
desc="Use the 60-30-10 rule, undertones and light direction to choose wall, fabric and accent colours that work together.",
cat="Colour", read=7, project="painting", body="""
<p>Paint is cheap; repainting isn't. These principles help you pick colours that look right in your light, with your floors and furniture.</p>
<h2>Start with what you can't change</h2><p>Flooring, kitchen counters, fireplace stone and large furniture all have undertones — warm (yellow, red, orange) or cool (blue, green, violet). Pick wall colours whose undertones agree with them.</p>
<h2>The 60-30-10 rule</h2><ul><li><strong>60%</strong> dominant colour: walls, large rugs.</li><li><strong>30%</strong> secondary: upholstery, curtains, an accent wall.</li><li><strong>10%</strong> accent: cushions, art, accessories.</li></ul><p>Our <a href="../tools.html#palette">palette generator</a> builds a 60-30-10 scheme from any base colour.</p>
<h2>Read your light</h2><ul><li><strong>North-facing rooms</strong> (northern hemisphere) get cool, indirect light — warmer whites and earthy tones balance them.</li><li><strong>South-facing rooms</strong> are bright and warm — cooler tones stay fresh.</li><li><strong>East</strong> is warm in the morning, cool later; <strong>west</strong> the reverse.</li></ul>
<h2>Classic harmony models</h2><ul><li><strong>Monochromatic:</strong> shades of one hue — calm and sophisticated.</li><li><strong>Analogous:</strong> neighbours on the colour wheel — relaxed and natural.</li><li><strong>Complementary:</strong> opposites — energetic; use one as the small accent.</li><li><strong>Triadic:</strong> three evenly spaced hues — playful; mute two of them.</li></ul>
<h2>Test before you commit</h2><ol><li>Paint large swatches (at least 2 × 2 ft) or use peel-and-stick samples.</li><li>Put them on two different walls.</li><li>Look morning, afternoon and night for two days.</li><li>Check them next to your flooring and fabrics.</li></ol>
<h2>Finish guide</h2><ul><li>Matte / flat: ceilings, low-traffic walls — hides flaws.</li><li>Eggshell: living spaces — slight sheen, wipeable.</li><li>Satin: kitchens, bathrooms, kids' rooms.</li><li>Semi-gloss: trim, doors, cabinets.</li></ul>
<p>Work out how much to buy with the <a href="../tools.html#paint">paint calculator</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="small-space-storage", title="Small Apartment Storage: 21 Ideas That Free Up Real Floor Space",
desc="Practical, renter-friendly storage strategies for studios and small apartments — vertical, hidden and multi-use.",
cat="Small Spaces", read=8, project="small-space", body="""
<p>In a small home, every square foot of floor is premium. The trick is to store vertically, hide what's ugly and choose furniture that does two jobs.</p>
<h2>Go vertical</h2><ol><li>Floor-to-ceiling shelving on one wall instead of several low units.</li><li>A shelf 12–18&quot; above door frames for rarely used items.</li><li>Pegboards or rail systems in the kitchen and entry.</li><li>Tall, narrow cabinets in bathroom corners.</li><li>Wall-mounted drop-leaf tables and desks.</li></ol>
<h2>Use hidden volume</h2><ol start="6"><li>Bed risers or a storage bed with drawers or a gas-lift base.</li><li>An ottoman or bench with a lift-up lid.</li><li>Under-sink pull-out organisers.</li><li>Toe-kick drawers under kitchen cabinets.</li><li>Over-the-door organisers for shoes, cleaning supplies or pantry items.</li></ol>
<h2>Furniture that multitasks</h2><ol start="11"><li>A sofa bed or daybed for guests.</li><li>Extendable dining tables.</li><li>Nesting side tables.</li><li>A console that works as a desk.</li><li>A room-divider bookcase that defines a sleeping zone in a studio.</li></ol>
<h2>Systems that keep it tidy</h2><ol start="16"><li>One-in, one-out for clothing and kitchenware.</li><li>Matching bins and labels — visual calm makes rooms feel bigger.</li><li>A landing zone by the door for keys, mail and bags.</li><li>Seasonal rotation: store off-season items in vacuum bags.</li><li>Digitise paper.</li><li>A five-minute nightly reset.</li></ol>
<h2>Plan it before you buy</h2><ol start="21"><li>Map storage in the <a href="../planner.html">room planner</a> so it doesn't block walkways — keep at least 30–36&quot; clear in front of drawers and doors.</li></ol>
<div class="callout"><strong>Renting?</strong> Freestanding units, tension rods, adhesive hooks and over-the-door systems give you storage without losing your deposit.</div>
<p>Need custom built-ins? <a href="../get-quote.html?project=small-space">Get quotes from carpenters and designers</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="open-plan-zoning", title="How to Zone an Open-Plan Space So Every Area Has a Purpose",
desc="Use rugs, lighting, furniture placement and materials to divide an open-plan living, dining and kitchen area.",
cat="Layout", read=7, project="living", body="""
<p>Open-plan homes are bright and sociable, but without zoning they feel like a furniture showroom. Zoning gives each activity its own place while keeping the openness.</p>
<h2>1. Define zones by activity</h2><p>List what happens in the space — cooking, eating, lounging, working, kids' play — and give each a footprint on your plan. Keep noisy zones (TV, kitchen) apart from quiet ones (reading, work) where possible.</p>
<h2>2. Anchor each zone with a rug</h2><p>A rug is the cheapest wall you can build. Size each rug to its furniture group, and leave at least 12–18&quot; of floor between rugs so zones read as separate.</p>
<h2>3. Use furniture as walls</h2><ul><li>Float the sofa with its back to the dining area.</li><li>Place a console or low bookcase behind it.</li><li>Use open shelving as a see-through divider.</li></ul>
<h2>4. Light each zone differently</h2><p>A pendant over the dining table, a floor lamp by the reading chair, under-cabinet lights in the kitchen. When different zones are lit independently, the room changes mood with the activity.</p>
<h2>5. Change materials or colour</h2><p>A painted ceiling over the dining area, a different floor finish in the kitchen, or wallpaper on one wall all mark boundaries without blocking light.</p>
<h2>6. Keep a clear circulation spine</h2><p>Leave a continuous 36&quot; (91 cm) path through the space. Zones sit on either side of it; nobody should walk through a conversation to reach the kitchen.</p>
<h2>7. Unify with a palette</h2><p>Zones should feel related. Repeat two or three colours and one wood tone throughout — the <a href="../tools.html#palette">palette generator</a> helps.</p>
<p>Get a professional zoning plan: <a href="../get-quote.html?project=living">match with a designer</a>.</p>
"""),
]
